I. PREFACE: THE PRECISION BARRIER
In terahertz-scale (1.0 THz) spintronics, traditional electron-beam lithography (EBL) and photolithography suffer from stochastic edge roughness and throughput limitations. The Hemminki DNF-Process bypasses these barriers by utilizing a physical, non-wearing atomic template to achieve absolute geometric fidelity in Antiferromagnetic (AFM) logic circuits.

II. TECHNICAL SPECIFICATIONS OF THE DNF
1. THE MONOCRYSTALLINE DIAMOND MOLD (MDM)
Unlike polymer or silicon-based masks, the DNF utilizes a monocrystalline diamond substrate as the master mold.

    Atomic Rigidity: Diamond’s Young’s modulus (E ≈ 1200 GPa) ensures zero elastic deformation during the imprint cycle. The linear deviation is defined as: δL = L₀(P/E) ≈ 0.
    Zero-Wear Interface: The carbon-lattice density prevents atom-loss or surface degradation, ensuring that the 1,000,000th imprint is identical to the 1st at a sub-nanometer scale.
    Thermal Conductance: Operating as a massive heat sink during the imprint, the MDM prevents thermal expansion of the substrate, locking the Phase-Length of the XOR-gates.

2. THERMAL-MOLECULAR BONDING (POLTTOSAUMAUS)
The DNF process is not a simple mechanical press; it is a high-energy structural integration.

    Mechanism: Following the mechanical imprint, a localized thermal pulse (Plasma or Laser-assisted) is applied to the track boundaries.
    Edge Passivation: This "welding" effect fuses the AFM thin film (e.g., NiO) to the track walls at a molecular level.
    Pinning Elimination: By creating an atomically smooth boundary, the DNF eliminates "Pinning Sites" (stochastic defects). The skyrmion motion follows the Thiele-formalism: G × v + αD · v = ∇U. The DNF-process forces ∇U → 0 at boundaries, allowing a constant velocity: V_afm = 1000 m/s without jitter.

3. THE GEOMETRIC DETERMINISM (GD)
The DNF is specifically engineered to produce the Diamond Interference Gate (DIG) geometry.

    Phase Calibration: The DNF guarantees a static π-phase shift (180.00°) by ensuring the path length difference (ΔL) between Input A and Input B is physically fixed to ΔL = λ_afm / 2.
    Repeatability: GD-factor = 1 - (σ_stochastic / L_target) > 99.9999%, enabling the mass production of coherent logic fabrics where every gate functions with identical latency: τ = L_gate / V_afm = 0.5 ps.

